# p_a1b2 — The standard split: commuter and work-from-home, quiet evenings

Two young adults share a flat. Marco (late twenties) works in a downtown office, leaving around 08:00 and returning around 17:30 on weekdays. Omar (late twenties) works from home at desk_o1 from 09:00 to 17:30, never leaving the flat during the workday. On weekends both sleep in until about 09:00, run errands together around 12:00–14:00, and are home the rest of the day.

The evening routine is shared: they cook together in the kitchen 18:00–19:00, eat at the kitchen table, then move to the living room for TV 19:30–22:00 (remote, blanket, snack bowl, mugs on the coffee table). Marco sometimes knits or plays guitar after dinner; Omar sometimes sketches. Bedtime is around 23:00.

What sets this hypothesis apart: nothing leaves the house except keys, wallets, and water bottles with Marco on weekday mornings. Omar never leaves during the workday. The laptop_omar stays at desk_o1 all work hours. The 03:00 patrol should show all objects in their walkthrough positions with only minor shifts (Omar's laptop back at desk_o1 rather than coffee_table).

What would refute it: Omar's keys, wallet, or water_bottle_omar sighted OUT_OF_HOUSE on a weekday between 09:00 and 17:00; or Marco's laptop sighted at desk_o1 (implying he works from the shared office).

